;;; `std.arith` — a name stated twice, with two numbers. M1f S1f.6.12.
;;;
;;; ── What the theory promises ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A `let` the theory cannot read is flagged as the `let`, `(malformed (let
;;; NAME NUMBER))`, whether or not a request uses the name, and `malformed-
;;; is-false` derives `(false)`. `let-twice` joins two `let`s on the name,
;;; which the fact index keys, and compares the two numbers by a guard. Until
;;; S1f.6.12 this program had two answers to its one request, 6 and 8, and no
;;; word.
;;;
;;; ── What the expectation means ─────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `(solve :expect (false))`: root is dead and R is empty. Both `let`s are
;;; flagged, each by the other, and the unsat core is the two of them.
;;; `34_let_twice_the_same.ein` is the case that is fine.

(import std.arith :symbols (arith let n add))

(let price (n z 5))
(let price (n z 7))
(add price (n z 1))

(solve :expect (false))
